/-! # Arithmetic of function fields and embedded finitely generated fields

Source: Stacks Project, Curves, Tag 0BY1 (theorem-curves-rational-maps), first and
third proof paragraphs; Varieties, Tag 0B1C (item-dimension-irreducible-trdeg);
Fields, Tags 030H and 09GH (blueprint: lem-sfb-curve-function-field-arithmetic).

The two statements record the scheme assertion and the independent field assertion.
The scalar map on the generic stalk pulls back constants along the structure morphism.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An integral scheme of finite type over a field has a finitely generated function
field, whose transcendence degree is its topological Krull dimension. The common
finite value is expressed by a natural number to compare the two dimension codomains.

Source: Stacks Project, Curves, Tag 0BY1, first proof paragraph; Varieties, Tag 0B1C,
item-dimension-irreducible-trdeg (blueprint: lem-sfb-curve-function-field-arithmetic).

Proof (node steps 1--2): choose a nonempty affine chart Spec A in a finite affine cover.
The finite-type hypothesis writes A as k[a₁,...,aᵣ]. By
lem-sc-integral-function-field-affine-fraction the generic stalk is Frac A, with its
canonical germ map. Every fraction g/h is in the field generated by the germs of the
finite algebra generators, proving field finite generation. Apply
lem-sc-closed-irreducible-dimension-trdeg to the whole integral scheme, with its generic
point. The generic stalk is a field, hence has zero maximal ideal; its residue field
(def-weil-residue-field) is canonically the same field over k. Polynomial relations
are preserved and reflected by this isomorphism, so its transcendence degree agrees
with that of the generic stalk. This gives the common natural-number dimension. -/
theorem functionField_finitelyGenerated_and_trdeg_eq_dimension
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (T : Scheme.{u}) [IsIntegral T]
    (sT : T ⟶ Spec (.of k)) [LocallyOfFiniteType sT] [QuasiCompact sT] :
    @IntermediateField.FG k _ T.functionField _
      (((Scheme.ΓSpecIso (.of k)).inv ≫ sT.appTop ≫
        T.presheaf.germ ⊤ (genericPoint T) trivial).hom.toAlgebra) ⊤ ∧
    ∃ d : ℕ, topologicalKrullDim T = (d : WithBot ℕ∞) ∧
      @Algebra.trdeg k T.functionField _ _
        (((Scheme.ΓSpecIso (.of k)).inv ≫ sT.appTop ≫
          T.presheaf.germ ⊤ (genericPoint T) trivial).hom.toAlgebra) = (d : Cardinal) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry

namespace FieldExtension

/-- Every base-field embedding between finitely generated extensions of the same finite
transcendence degree makes the target finite over the image. No separability is required.

Source: Stacks Project, Fields, Tags 030H (lemma-transcendence-degree-tower) and
09GH (lemma-algebraic-finitely-generated); Curves, Tag 0BY1, third proof paragraph
(blueprint: lem-sfb-curve-function-field-arithmetic).

Proof (node steps 3--4): set E = φ(K). The embedding identifies K and E over k and
preserves and reflects all polynomial relations, so trdeg_k E = d. By
lem-kun-transcendence-degree-tower, d = d + trdeg_E L. The latter cardinal is finite,
and cancellation in the natural numbers gives trdeg_E L = 0. The zero-degree criterion
of the same node makes L/E algebraic. A finite tuple generating L over k also generates
it over E, since k is contained in E. Each generator is algebraic over E, and
lem-weil-pbc-algebraic-generators-finite-extension makes L/E finite. None of these
steps assumes separability, and cancellation includes d = 0. -/
theorem finite_image_of_finitelyGenerated_of_trdeg_eq
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (L : Type w) [Field L] [Algebra k L]
    (hK : (⊤ : IntermediateField k K).FG) (hL : (⊤ : IntermediateField k L).FG)
    (d : ℕ) (hKd : Algebra.trdeg k K = (d : Cardinal.{v}))
    (hLd : Algebra.trdeg k L = (d : Cardinal.{w})) (φ : K →ₐ[k] L) :
    Module.Finite φ.fieldRange L := by
  sorry

end FieldExtension
